@meta
id: 30-12
title: When a language server is worth it
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: lsp-setup
par: 220
minutes: 9
vscode: nothing to do — the extension is the server
open: lsp.vim

@brief
Everything so far has been the poor man's version, and the poor man's
version is genuinely good. Here is what it cannot do, and what fixes it.

A tags file knows names and places. A language server knows types. That
one difference is where the last three lessons hurt: the tag list with
two `refund` entries, the rename that hit another class and a routing
key, the errors that needed a build to appear at all. A server resolves
the symbol under your cursor to one declaration, so it gives one
definition, renames only true references, and reports diagnostics as you
type without running `tsc`.

Vim 9 does not ship a client. `:h lsp` describes the protocol and points
at the plugins that speak it — `yegappan/lsp` is the common vim 9 one,
`prabirshrestha/vim-lsp` the older vimscript one. What follows is the
whole configuration for TypeScript, and it is short.

`lsp.vim` is open with its first line written. Add, exactly:

    call LspAddServer([#{name: 'tsserver', filetype: ['typescript'], path: 'typescript-language-server', args: ['--stdio']}])
    nnoremap gd :LspGotoDefinition<CR>
    nnoremap gr :LspRename<CR>

Nothing runs here: there is no server installed and no network. The file
is the deliverable.

@keys
| `:h lsp` | vim's own account of what a language server is |
| `packadd name` | load a plugin from `pack/*/opt` now, rather than at startup |
| `LspAddServer` | register a server: which filetypes, which binary, which arguments |
| `:LspGotoDefinition` `:LspRename` `:LspHover` | the three you will actually press |
| `:LspDiagnostics` | the errors, into the location list |

@hints
`G` to the last line, `o` to open a line under it, and type the three
lines with Enter between them.
--
`#{...}` is vim 9's literal dictionary: keys without quotes. The square
brackets around it are because `LspAddServer` takes a list of servers —
you would add `pylsp` and `gopls` to the same call.
--
The mappings deliberately shadow `gd` and `gr`. When the server is
running you want the server's answer; when it is not, the built-in `gd`
is still there and still works.

@solution
    G o <the three lines, Enter between them> <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Worth it when: the project is one language, it is already on your disk
with its dependencies installed, and you work in it every day. Rename
and go-to-definition stop being approximate, and diagnostics arrive
before you save. For a monorepo you touch daily, the setup cost is an
hour and it pays back in a week.

Not worth it when: you are on a box you ssh into, the repo is thirty
gigabytes, the project is four languages, `node_modules` is not
installed, or you are reading code rather than writing it. A server
needs a working project to be useful. `ctags -R src` needs a directory.

The two are not rivals, and most people who set up a server keep a tags
file as well — for the languages the server does not cover, for the
vendored code it refuses to index, and for the first thirty seconds
after opening vim while the server is still starting up.

The real reason to understand the tags version is that it is the one
that is always available. The keys you learned this chapter — `Ctrl-]`,
`Ctrl-T`, `Ctrl-W }`, `:cn` — are the same keys whatever is answering
the question. Point them at a server if you have one.

@start lsp.vim
packadd lsp

@expected lsp.vim
packadd lsp
call LspAddServer([#{name: 'tsserver', filetype: ['typescript'], path: 'typescript-language-server', args: ['--stdio']}])
nnoremap gd :LspGotoDefinition<CR>
nnoremap gr :LspRename<CR>
